    <item>
      <title>The Velocity Conundrum</title>
      <link>/2015/12/25/the-velocity-conundrum/</link>
      <pubDate>Fri, 25 Dec 2015 02:13:50 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>/2015/12/25/the-velocity-conundrum/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;&#xA;  &lt;figure&gt;&#xA;    &lt;img src=&#34;/images/general/singapore-street.jpg&#34; alt=&#34;Near Chinatown street in Singapore&#34; &gt;&#xA;    &lt;figcaption &#xA;    &#x9;style=&#34; &#xA;    &#x9;font-style: italic; &#xA;    &#x9;text-align: center; &#xA;    &#x9;color: grey; &#xA;    &#x9;font-size: 14px&#34;&#xA;    &#x9;&gt;&#xA;    &#x9;Near Chinatown street in Singapore&#xA;    &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#xA;  &lt;/figure&gt;&#xA;&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;Velocity! It&amp;rsquo;s a term that has potentially confounded, frustrated and perhaps amused Agile practitioners and customers alike at some point. It&amp;rsquo;s a concept that can be quite helpful though, when used accurately as a planning &amp;amp; estimation tool.&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Velocity is a measure of the number of story points delivered in an Iteration&lt;/em&gt;. The word &lt;strong&gt;delivered&lt;/strong&gt; here means - dev &amp;amp; test complete, and&#xA;ready for Production. Depending on your organizational maturity it could also mean &amp;ndash; &lt;strong&gt;in Production&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
